# 9. FUNCTION CALL CONCEPT

Suppose:

```text
main()
  ↓
function()
```

When `function()` executes, the system maintains information necessary to return to the caller.

Simplified:

```text
Caller
  ↓
Function
  ↓
Return to caller
```

A memory corruption vulnerability can interfere with this process.

---

# 10. CONTROL FLOW

A program normally follows:

```text
Instruction A
     ↓
Instruction B
     ↓
Instruction C
     ↓
Return
```

A serious memory corruption vulnerability may cause:

```text
Instruction A
     ↓
Corrupted control data
     ↓
Unexpected execution location
```

This is why exploit developers care about control-flow information.

---

# 11. INSTRUCTION POINTER

The CPU maintains a register representing the location of the next instruction to execute.

Names differ by architecture:

### x86

```text
EIP
```

### x86-64

```text
RIP
```

Conceptually:

```text
RIP/EIP
   ↓
Next instruction
```

If a memory corruption vulnerability allows an attacker to influence this value, control-flow corruption may become possible.

